+<a name="repeatcycle"> </a>
+<h3>Repeating cycles</h3>
+<p>
+Some dosage courses repeat in cycles. For example, an oral contraceptive is taken once daily for 21 days, followed by 7 days without tablets, and then the cycle starts again. This is represented with <code>repeatCycle</code>: the duration of one cycle (28 days in this example). The steps describe one cycle (one step with a component taken once daily, bounded to 21 days), and the whole course starts again from the first step when the cycle duration has passed, measured from the start of the course. The time between the end of the last step and the end of the cycle is a period without doses. See <a href="dosage-examples.html">Example 5</a> on the examples page.
+</p>
+<p>
+<code>repeatCycle</code> has the following limitations:
+</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>It repeats the whole dosage course: all the steps, together. It cannot make a single step repeat in a cycle of its own (<code>step.count</code> repeats a step a number of times, one after the other).</li>
+  <li>The cycle is expected to be at least as long as the steps it contains. If the steps take longer than the cycle, the meaning is not defined.</li>
+  <li>It does not say how many cycles are taken, or when to stop. That follows from the context, such as the validity of the prescription or a stop instruction, and can be stated in <code>renderedInstruction</code>.</li>
+  <li>Days on which a placebo or another tablet is taken are not days without doses: they are represented as a step (or component) with that dose, not as the remainder of the cycle.</li>
+</ul>
+
 <p>
 DosageDetails is used in the following places: [%dtusage DosageDetails%]
 </p>
